From f4f31c342e2ed4ba8e8e8fbf3a7fba964d779d76 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nicolas Villar <15879262+nvillar@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 00:06:05 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] PaneManager: classify single, gapless and spanned displays
 like DuoPanelGeometry

The pane states derived in 0013 treated any display longer than one panel as spanned: max(width, height) > panel. A
single screen in phone mode is panel x height (Duo 1350x1800, Duo 2 1344x1892), so its long side is the panel height
and it was reported as spanned, with an occupied second pane off the right edge of the display. The rects were also
always in natural (rotation 0) coordinates, so they did not match the display once rotated.

Move the arithmetic to PaneGeometry (no android.* classes), which mirrors the mode and layout semantics of the
framework's DuoPanelGeometry (hidden framework code, not linkable from this sdk_version: "current" library):

- spanned (2 * panel + hinge long) and gapless (2 * panel long, persist.sys.phh.duo.disable_hinge=1, detected from the
  display size like HingeMath.isGapless): two occupied panes, pane 0 in focus;
- single panel, either orientation: pane 0 is the whole display, pane 1 is empty and unoccupied (the array keeps two
  entries so callers that index pane 1 keep working; which physical screen is lit cannot be told from the size);
- any other size (scaled or letterboxed display) or device: no panes;
- rects follow the current display rotation, pane 0 tracking the physical left panel.

Microsoft Launcher keeps its intentional no-connect behaviour. Host test surface-sdk/test/PaneGeometryTest.java
(same vectors as DuoPanelGeometryTest plus Duo 1 rotations and the phone-mode regression).

Co-authored-by: Copilot App <223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>
---
